<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pattadakal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pattadakal is a village on the Malaprabha river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagalkot-district">Bagalkot district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with a group of 7th- and 8th-century temples built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>. Used for royal coronations, it combines northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a> and southern Dravida styles; the largest, the Virupaksha temple, was built by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokamahadevi">Lokamahadevi</a> around 740. The group is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pattadakal.jpg" alt="Pattadakal" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nithin bolar k · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pattadakal_000.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pattadakal, also called Raktapura, stands on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaprabha-river">Malaprabha River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagalkot-district">Bagalkot district</a>, about 23 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and about 9.7 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, both important centres of Chalukya monuments. The river turns northwards a kilometre before it reaches the site, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition a north-flowing river is called Uttarvahini Ganga. The place was considered holy for this reason. Its name means stone of coronation, and Chalukya kings held coronation ceremonies there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapida">Vinayaditya</a> in the 7th century CE. It was also known as Kisuvolal, the valley of red soil, and Ptolemy refers to it as Petirgal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early Chalukya rulers of the 5th and 6th centuries were followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> who later became followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, so the temples are generally dedicated to Shiva. There are ten major temples, nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and one Jain, with many small shrines. They are built of local sandstone, and some sculpture is of polished black granite. Four temples are in the Chalukya Dravida style of the south, four in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Nagara</a> style of the north, and the Papanatha temple fuses the two.</p><p>The oldest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples is the Sangameshwara, built under Vijayaditya Satyashraya and left unfinished at his death. The Virupaksha temple, the largest and most sophisticated, was sponsored by Queen Lokmahadevi after the campaigns of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-ii">Vikramaditya II</a> against the Pallavas. Its inscription names the chief architect, Gunda Anivaritacharya, and artists' names are inscribed beneath the panels they worked on.</p><p>The nearby Trailokeshwara temple was sponsored by Queen Trailokyamahadevi. The last temple is the Jain Narayana temple, probably of the 9th century and possibly sponsored by the Rashtrakuta king Krishna II. Inscriptions in old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> record grants by kings and queens, and one 8th-century column carries both the Siddhamatrika and Kannada scripts.</p><p>The friezes show scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a>. Art historian Cathleen Cummings reads the sculpture as an expression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingship and of the tension between the householder's life and that of the renouncing monk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pattadakal is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has described as a harmonious blend of architectural forms from northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is protected under Indian law and managed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>. The Virupaksha temple is still an active place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> worship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pattadakal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal women were prominent patrons; the queen Vijayabhattarika issued grants in her own name, and queens of Vikramaditya II built the principal temples at <b>Pattadakal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
